The only real translation challenge was the [38;5;10mgoto[0m structure: the C body has a forward jump to [38;5;10mlabel2[0m (skipping the [38;5;10mx[0m block) and a backward jump to [38;5;10mlabel1[0m from the bottom, plus a [38;5;10mcontinue[0m that re-tests the [38;5;10mwhile[0m condition. I modeled this as an inner [38;5;10mloop[0m with an explicit entry point ([38;5;10mLabel1[0m or [38;5;10mLabel2[0m) per pass, and a labeled [38;5;10mcontinue 'while_loop[0m for the C [38;5;10mcontinue[0m. That reproduces the flow exactly rather than approximating it — e.g. [38;5;10mdriver(1, 4)[0m prints [38;5;10mloop y x y y y[0m, where the leading [38;5;10my[0m before [38;5;10mx[0m only happens because of the forward goto.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Notes on fidelity:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- Output goes through C [38;5;10mprintf[0m (declared as an extern variadic) rather than Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m, so stdout buffering and interleaving with any other C output in the host process are unchanged.[0m[0m
- The header has no namespace macro, so the linker symbol is plain [38;5;10mdriver[0m; confirmed with [38;5;10mnm[0m.[0m[0m
- Decrements use [38;5;10mwrapping_sub[0m. Negative [38;5;10my[0m with the original loop runs forever in C (signed overflow is UB there), so I kept the infinite loop instead of "fixing" it; those cases were verified by comparing bounded output prefixes, which are identical.[0m[0m
